WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.703
Imagine you’re tracking deliveries across two shipping companies. You want an

00:00:04.703 --> 00:00:09.956
assistant to spot the problems that need attention. But it should only use information

00:00:09.956 --> 00:00:11.300
you’re allowed to see.

00:00:14.802 --> 00:00:20.895
A lens defines the slice of information someone is allowed to use. Here, the delivery

00:00:20.895 --> 00:00:25.627
platform shares shipment details, but keeps internal costs hidden.

00:00:29.127 --> 00:00:34.506
The platform also decides which companies get that view. Here, an active Pro

00:00:34.506 --> 00:00:39.602
subscription is required. Each company gets access to its own shipments.

00:00:43.104 --> 00:00:49.315
Andes can share a smaller view with its customers. Here, that means Express deliveries

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:54.154
for eligible customers. The limits set by the platform still apply.

00:00:57.655 --> 00:01:03.143
Sofia has access to both shipping companies. Mateo has different access. Switch

00:01:03.143 --> 00:01:08.355
between them, and the information available changes with the person asking.

00:01:11.856 --> 00:01:17.463
Which deliveries need attention? A rule turns that question into something precise.

00:01:17.463 --> 00:01:22.867
Before it runs, the system checks that it only uses information this customer is

00:01:22.867 --> 00:01:23.881
allowed to see.

00:01:27.381 --> 00:01:33.221
One long delay and several repeated delays can point to different problems. We can ask

00:01:33.221 --> 00:01:37.431
about either. The answers come from the same shipment history.

00:01:42.827 --> 00:01:48.306
We can even ask: was the second-longest hold serious, too? That helps separate a

00:01:48.306 --> 00:01:50.702
recurring problem from one bad day.

00:01:54.202 --> 00:02:00.037
Now read the notes. This shipment has cleared customs, but nobody agrees who should

00:02:00.037 --> 00:02:05.871
arrange collection. Another is waiting for a required lab result. Both are delayed.

00:02:05.871 --> 00:02:08.752
Only one needs someone to take ownership.

00:02:12.254 --> 00:02:18.220
That gives us a useful label: an ownership handoff. We can inspect the notes behind it

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:24.186
before trusting the interpretation. The label has an expiry time, so an old conclusion

00:02:24.186 --> 00:02:26.129
doesn’t stay around forever.

00:02:29.631 --> 00:02:35.468
Now combine the delivery details with that interpretation. Show Express shipments with

00:02:35.468 --> 00:02:41.306
repeated holds and an ownership problem. We can put those conditions in a single rule.

00:02:44.806 --> 00:02:50.813
Two shipments match. Another question, about the second-longest hold, leaves just one.

00:02:50.813 --> 00:02:54.656
You can see exactly which delivery needs a closer look.

00:02:58.155 --> 00:03:04.230
Now expire the labels. That match disappears. The next answer needs fresh evidence.

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:13.171
What if we ask for something outside our access? Internal costs are refused. Asking

00:03:13.171 --> 00:03:17.105
for another customer’s shipments doesn’t give us their data.

00:03:20.606 --> 00:03:26.453
Access can change, too. Remove a company’s eligibility, and its shipments disappear.

00:03:26.453 --> 00:03:30.631
The earlier answer is marked out of date until we ask again.

00:03:34.133 --> 00:03:40.595
These definitions are written in JSON: a text format that people and software can both

00:03:40.595 --> 00:03:46.682
read. A person or an assistant can propose a change. The same access limits still

00:03:46.682 --> 00:03:47.133
apply.
